{"name":"Diphenhydramine / doxylamine sleep aids","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Diphenhydramine and the related doxylamine are first-generation antihistamines that cause drowsiness and are the active ingredients in many nonprescription sleep aids. People use them to fall asleep faster, which looksmaxxers link to skin and facial recovery.","Diphenhydramine blocks histamine H1 receptors and crosses the blood-brain barrier, producing drowsiness, and is also a strong muscarinic antagonist. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine's 2017 guideline recommended against it for insomnia, citing poor effectiveness and low-quality evidence, and a 2022 Lancet network meta-analysis found little evidence to guide its use."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["They are taken as over-the-counter tablets or capsules at bedtime for sleep onset, usually occasionally. Effects of drowsiness come within an hour or so."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Reported effects include dizziness, dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, and at higher doses confusion, hallucinations and delirium; overdose can cause arrhythmia. Anticholinergic drug use in later life has been linked to cognitive decline, though a 2019 study found no significant association for antihistamines specifically."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Diphenhydramine_2D_skeletal.svg/960px-Diphenhydramine_2D_skeletal.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Diphenhydramine_2D_skeletal.svg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Diphenhydramine 2D skeletal","license":"Public domain","creator":"Fvasconcellos (talk · contribs)","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diphenhydramine_2D_skeletal.svg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Diphenhydramine-trans.svg/960px-Diphenhydramine-trans.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Diphenhydramine-trans.svg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Diphenhydramine-trans","license":"CC BY 3.0","creator":"Benrr101","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diphenhydramine-trans.svg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Diphenhydramine_2_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Diphenhydramine_2_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c8/Diphenhydramine_2_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Diphenhydramine 2 (cropped)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","creator":"Mark Oniffrey","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diphenhydramine_2_(cropped).jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Diphenhydramine_1_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Diphenhydramine_1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/16/Diphenhydramine_1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Diphenhydramine 1 (cropped)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","creator":"Mark Oniffrey","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diphenhydramine_1_(cropped).jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Diphenhydramine_%281%29.JPG/960px-Diphenhydramine_%281%29.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/17/Diphenhydramine_%281%29.JPG?